{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Reuben, you are my firstborn, etc.</b> Its Targum: Behold, it is fitting for you to receive three portions — the birthright, the priesthood, and the kingship. He means to explain the verse: 'Reuben, you are my firstborn' — you are fit for the birthright, because you are 'my might and the first of my strength'; and you are fit for the priesthood, which is 'the excellence of dignity,' for the divine service belonged to the firstborn; and you are fit for the kingship, 'the excellence of power.' However, 'unstable as water, you shall not excel,' for 'a king by justice establishes the land' (Proverbs 29:4), and every judge must be measured in the matter of judgment, but you were unstable as water in all your affairs, and 'unstable' means hasty. Therefore you are not fit to receive the preeminence of kingship. And 'because you went up to your father's bed' — therefore you are not fit for the priesthood, for the priesthood's concern is to cause the Shekhinah to rest in the Temple, which is called 'your father's bed' that is in heaven — as Rashi explains on the verse 'Behold, his bed, which is Solomon's' (Song of Songs 3:7) and on the verse 'He lodges between the breasts' (Song of Songs 1:13), and as they said, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 7a): 'When I was beloved, I would sleep on the breast of the mighty one at the head of the staircase,' etc. And you caused the departure of the Shekhinah through the matter of Bilhah; therefore you are not fit for the priesthood. And he used the plural 'beds' (מִשְׁכְּבֵי), for there will be three Temples in the future, besides the Tabernacle." "<b>Then you defiled my couch — he went up.</b> At that same time you also defiled my couch, that is, my bed, so that I would no longer beget sons from her, and you did this out of desire for wealth, so that you would have a large share in the inheritance. And so the Ramban explained; therefore I penalize you in money, so that you shall not have the legal right of the firstborn to take a double portion, and it shall belong to Joseph, who had a good eye and who provided for his father and his brothers — as it is written (1 Chronicles 5:1), 'and when he defiled his father's couch, his birthright was given to the sons of Joseph.'" "<b>The excellence of dignity, and the excellence of power.</b> The commentators raised a difficulty: according to the plain implication of the passage, he did not bless him at all, yet Scripture says, 'Every man according to his blessing he blessed them' (Genesis 49:28). And I say that Jacob blessed him from the wellspring of the blessings of Moses, of whom it is said (Deuteronomy 33:6), 'Let Reuben live, and not die, and let his men be a number.' And Maharsha explained that since Reuben was required to go at the head of the vanguard in war, and the majority of those killed in war are the ones in front, therefore he blessed him, 'Let Reuben live, and not die.' And it seems to me that he blessed him in the matter of might and strength, that he should be a mighty man in the land and give might and power to the people, for those who go at the head of the vanguard need to be mighty warriors; and every mighty man is called 'a living man of great deeds' (2 Samuel 23:20) — to exclude the weakling, who is considered as dead. Therefore it says, 'Let Reuben live.' And as for what is said, 'and let his men be a number,' it seems to me to explain that the victory over enemies by natural means depends on two things: either on a multitude of people, or on men of strength and might, and both together are best. And since Reuben caused the departure of the Shekhinah from Jacob, it stands to reason that his punishment would be measure for measure — that in his wars the Shekhinah would withdraw from him, and he would need to wage wars by natural means. Therefore he needed these two blessings: that his men be mighty warriors, and concerning this he said, 'Let Reuben live, and not die'; and that they also be numerous, so much so that they would need counting, for a small nation is called 'men of number' (Genesis 34:30) — whose number is known to the public — but a great nation needs to be counted, and concerning this he said, 'and let his men be a number,' for the expression 'let his men be' implies that they would be many and require counting. And so Jacob blessed him with these two blessings and gave him the 'dignity' and the 'power': the 'dignity' is the number, and this is precisely the blessing of 'and let his men be a number'; and the 'power' is the strength and might hinted at in the blessing of 'Let Reuben live, and not die.'" "And the matter of 'number' fits even better according to Rashi's explanation, who explained 'and let his men be a number' to mean that they would be counted together with the rest of the tribes. And since it says 'his men,' this implies that it speaks of many men who would be counted together with Israel whenever all Israel are counted for some purpose. And Jacob said: 'Behold, I am giving you the dignity and the power,' for the dignity concerns the counting, as it is written 'When you lift up the head' (Exodus 30:12), 'Lift up the head' (Numbers 1:2), and so everywhere the counting is mentioned in the language of lifting up. And Jacob said: Even though by strict justice you were worthy of 'excellence of dignity and power' — meaning that you should have superiority over all the tribes, double and twofold in the matter of dignity and power, for it would be fitting that from you there should be men of number twice as many as the rest, and likewise 'excellence of power' — it would have been fitting to give you might and strength more than all the tribes, to take a double portion in dignity and power — but because you were unstable as water in the sin of Bilhah, therefore 'you shall not excel' to have superiority over your brothers in dignity and power. Nevertheless, the substance of the dignity and the power I am giving you as a gift, for by strict justice the sin should have caused that you would have no dignity or power at all — you should not have had even an equal share with your brothers. Nevertheless, I hereby give you my blessing of peace, that you shall have dignity and power equal to your brothers — but 'you shall not excel,' to take superiority over your brothers, because the transgression causes this for you. And we may explain that the matter of the 'dignity' is that they would be counted in the tally of the rest of the tribes, as Rashi explained on 'and the sons of Jacob were twelve' (Genesis 35:23), and likewise in the verse 'and his men were a number' (Numbers 26:14), and all is explained along one path."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 49, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:34:34.514391696Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}